This is a CBP ruling on retractable screens (similar to Phantom Screens). The likely classification is 6303.12 or 6303.92 (curtains, drapes, interior blinds), or 3925.30 (shutters, blinds, and similar articles of plastics), or 5407/5903.

I recall CBP rulings on retractable screens classifying them in 6303.92.2030, "Curtains... interior blinds... knitted or crocheted... of synthetic fibers", or 6303.92.2030 for woven polyester fabric. Another possibility is 3925.30.5000, since the screen is a PVC-coated fabric. Window screens that retract are generally considered "blinds" and were classified in 6303.92.2030, which covers other, of synthetic fibers, not knitted, "Other." Rolls of PVC-coated polyester fabric fall under Chapter 59 (5903), but the made-up article is classified under 6303 as a made-up textile. Note 2 to Chapter 63 excludes Chapter 59 fabrics? Chapter 63 covers made-up articles of any textile fabric, including coated fabrics. However, the articles with significant hardware may be classified under 3925.30 as plastic shutters and blinds, but the essential character here is the textile panel, with 28% polyester and 72% PVC by weight. Since the plastic dominates by weight, 3925.30 is plausible. I believe the ruling went with 6303.12.0000 (knitted) or 6303.92.2030 (woven), so I choose 6303.92.2030.

{"hts_code": "6303.92.2030", "reason": "The retractable screen is a made-up woven polyester (PVC-coated) textile window covering functioning as an interior blind/curtain, classified in heading 6303 as other woven synthetic fiber articles."}
